{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795700026,"nonce":"FKeGbardU7frqBj+kEccMaguk9A=","title":"newton(direct=True) without a linear= slot factorizes with JAX spsolve, not lu()'s preferred backend","message":"`jno.solve.newton(direct=True)` with no `linear=` slot factorizes the assembled tangent with `sparse_lu_solve` (JAX `spsolve`). `jno.solve.lu()` with no backend picks a better one at solve time, and the Newton path never asks it.\n\n**What goes wrong.** `lu()` resolves its backend late: `_preferred_direct_backend()` returns `\"cudss\"` on a GPU with float64 and cuDSS available, else `\"device\"`. The comment there gives the measured cost of the alternative on GPU: cuSolver's `spsolve` is a QR, 280 ms against 11 ms at 7.6k P2 dofs, 2.4 s against 36 ms at 13k P1 elasticity dofs, over 240 s against 0.13 s at 49k P2 dofs. In `jno/solve.py` the `direct=True` branch of newton hands `linear_solve=None` to `newton_direct`, whose docstring says \"Default: `sparse_lu_solve`\". So a user who writes `newton(direct=True)` on a GPU gets the slow QR path, while the same user writing `linear=jno.solve.lu()` gets cuDSS (or PARDISO on the host).\n\n**Reproduce.** On a GPU in float64 with cuDSS installed, solve any nonlinear FEM problem with `fem.solve(nonlinear=jno.solve.newton(direct=True))` and again with `fem.solve(nonlinear=jno.solve.newton(direct=True), linear=jno.solve.lu())`. The first factorizes with spsolve, the second with cuDSS. Reading the code is enough to see the first: the default is hard-wired in `newton_direct`.\n\n**Expected.** `direct=True` means \"a direct factorization\"; with no slot it should use the same default `lu()` uses, since there is one definition of the preferred direct backend. The same gap was fixed for `staggered(direct=True)` in #220.\n\n**Where.** jno/solve.py (the `direct` branch of the newton builder, `linear_solve=linear_solve`); jno/utils/solver/newton_krylov.py `newton_direct` (default `sparse_lu_solve`); jno/_fem.py has the other hard-wired `sparse_lu_solve` calls (lines ~4318-4355) worth checking in the same pass.\n\n**Workaround.** Pass the slot explicitly: `linear=jno.solve.lu()` (or `lu(backend=\"cudss\")`).\n\n**Direction.** Resolve the default exactly as #220 did for staggered: when `direct=True` and no `linear=` is given, use `lu()` through `at_solve_spec`. Keep `sparse_lu_solve` as the fallback when the tangent is traced without a context. Measure one GPU Newton solve before and after.","files":null}]}